  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Mon Aug 17 2026\n\nToday-Tuesday:\nPlenty of sunshine early this afternoon, but instability is\nbuilding ahead of an area of showers and isolated thunderstorms\nassociated with a cold front in northwest Minnesota. Surface-\nbased CAPE from ~500-1500 J/kg with 0-6 km shear around 30-40 kt\nis becoming favorable for a few strong to low-end severe storms\nto develop. DCAPE is surprisingly not as impressive, topping out\nat 700 J/kg, but that could build a little more. Low-level lapse\nrates around 8 C/km are quite favorable for some surface-based\ndevelopment. With this, SPC has a marginal risk for severe\nweather across most of northeast Minnesota this afternoon and\nevening. With an ongoing cluster of showers and some rumbles of\nthunder in northwest Minnesota as the catalyst for this\nafternoon's development, expect this activity to spread\neast/southeast for the next few hours and potentially kick off\nadditional storms along/ahead of it. Storms may organize into a\nline, and that's primarily where the wind threat would come\nfrom. This does not look to be a widespread severe event by any\nmeans, but definitely a few strong to severe storms are possible\nwith widespread general showers and storms. The initial line\nshould pass through northwest Wisconsin through maybe 1-2 AM.\nBehind that, there could be some redevelopment of some showers\nalong the front (the storms this evening should outrun the front\nitself). Then, some decaying storms from Montana/South Dakota\ncould pass by to our south (though a slightly more northerly\ntrack could clip the Brainerd Lakes into northwest Wisconsin).\nFor most, it should be a pretty quiet day Tuesday with post-\nfrontal northwest flow.\n\nTuesday night - Thursday:\nHigh pressure is expected to be a dominant feature going into\nWednesday and Thursday. Light winds and clear skies Tuesday\nnight is looking to bring some pretty chilly lows. Lower 40s and\nupper 30s are expected especially in the usual cold spots. I\nwouldn't be surprised to hear of some very isolated frost in the\ncoldest of the cold spots (10% chance). Lows may be chilly again\nWednesday night, but increasing clouds may keep temperatures a\nfew degrees warmer than Tuesday night. Highs in the 70s are\nexpected Wednesday-Thursday. Some 80s are likely Thursday east-\ncentral Minnesota into northwest Wisconsin.\n\nThursday late Afternoon - Saturday:\nThere are some hints of a possible upper-level trough that could\npass through later Thursday afternoon into Thursday night. A\ncold front would likely pass through as well going into Friday\nat some point. Convective parameters still don't look overly\nimpressive for strong storms, but we'll keep an eye on it as\nthat could still change. Any rain chances through Friday look\npretty light overall as showers/storms will likely be pretty\nprogressive. Going into Friday night and Saturday, we'll most\nlikely end up with northwest flow. The upper level pattern looks\nlike it will prominently feature a ridge axis to our west with a\ntrough to the east following behind the cold front. Surface high\npressure will likely bring us into a pretty quiet weather\nweekend. There are some hints that some waves of cyclonic\nvorticity could advect through the region on Saturday attendant\nwith the broader trough to our northeast. Some wraparound\nshowers can't be ruled out in that case.",
